abstract: Cosmic matter forms concentrations whose external force can often be described by
  their mass and spatial moments. Encounters and material exchange can make finer structure
  relevant again. We determine when field and object descriptions preserve that force and the
  subsequent evolution of specified observables while retaining unresolved influence in residual
  variables and memory. The compact internal-space construction supplies the matter carriers
  and collective source. Their coupled variations determine the macroscopic evolution. The applications
  examine homogeneous response reuse and a constrained nonuniform Einstein–matter perturbation
  together with autonomous expansion from a local constitutive action. In the expanding root-mean-square
  (RMS) application, the endpoint amplitude response fixes the constitutive flux. Its potential
  determines both the cold-matter force and the binding term in the expansion constraint. A
  finite-interval enclosure establishes noncrossing, positive reduced kinetic curvature and
  regular weak-field material-clock readings. Reconstruction in a common state space combines
  object replacement with moving spectral boundaries and truncated memory for a finite reference
  evolution. Spatial moments bound the force error. Cloud-in-cell assignment admits an exact
  eight-moment cell representation. A positive lower bound on the source derivative of the stationary
  constraint bounds the error in reusing its response. Integrable stability and defect bounds
  on a certified state tube then control the accumulated reconstruction error. A common modulus
  of continuity transfers this bound to observables over the prescribed interval. The first
  accepted window is the least certified member of a finite ordered family. Finite Galerkin
  examples retain positive density and the prescribed accuracy with reduced arithmetic work
  including verification. A cosmological particle calculation encloses the continuous-time trajectory
  at fixed mesh resolution. Its weakly perturbed cold-sheet reconstruction also gives full-frequency
  force bounds and continuous position and momentum equation-defect bounds. These estimates
  select sufficient retained structure for the prescribed interaction and observables. They
  control the re-entry of finer coordinates.
